## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
Phos-Flur Rinse contains sodium fluoride at a concentration of 0.044% (w/v). How many milligrams of sodium fluoride are present in a 10 mL dose?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the mass of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose of Pos-Flf Rub, we use the given concentration:  
\[
\text{Concentration} = 0.044 \text{ w/v} = 0.044 \text{ g SO}_4\text{F} \text{ per } 100 \text{ mL}
\]  
First, convert the concentration to g SO\(4\text{F}\) per mL:  
\[
\frac{0.044 \text{ g SO}_4\text{F}}{100 \text{ mL}} = 0.00044 \text{ g SO}_4\text{F} \text{ per mL}
\]  
Now, multiply this by the volume of the dose (10 mL) to find the mass of sodium fluoride in the dose:  
\[
0.00044 \text{ g SO}_4\text{F} \text{ per mL} \times 10 \text{ mL} = 0.0044 \text{ g SO}_4\text{F}
\]  
Finally, convert the mass from grams to milligrams:  
\[
0.0044 \text{ g} \times 1000 \text{ mg/g} = 4.4 \text{ mg SO}_4\text{F}
\]  
Thus, there are \(\boxed{4.4 \text{ mg}}\) of sodium fluoride in a 10 mL dose.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

