","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΔS°(products) - ΔS°(reactants)

where ΔS° is the standard entropy change for each substance.

The reaction is:

CO(g) + 1/2 O₂(g) → CO₂(g)

Since we have 3 moles of CO burning, the correct equation is:

3CO(g) + 3/2 O₂(g) → 3CO₂(g)

From standard thermodynamic tables, we can look up the standard entropies (S°) at 298 K for each compound:

S°(CO(g)) = 20.1 J/mol·K
S°(O₂(g)) = 20.6 J/mol·K
S°(CO₂(g)) = 237.5 J/mol·K

Now, we can calculate the change in entropy:

ΔS = 3 * S°(CO₂) - (3 * S°(CO) + 3/2 * S°(O₂))

ΔS = 3 * 237.5 J/mol·K - (3 * 20.1 J/mol·K + 3/2 * 20.6 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 712.5 J/mol·K - (60.3 J/mol·K + 30.9 J/mol·K)

ΔS = 712.5 J/mol·K - 91.2 J/mol·K

ΔS = 621.3 J/mol·K

