","The concentration and duration of exposure of a photosensitizer are two key factors that significantly affect the rate of photochemical reactions. The rate of these reactions is not linear with both factors. The rate of photochemical reactions involving photosensitmitters is governed by the following equation:

\[
\text{Rate} = \text{k} \times [\text{Reactant}] \times [\text{Photosensitizer}] \times \text{Intensity} \times \text{Duration}
\]

where:
- Rate is the rate of the photochemical reaction
- k is the rate constant
- [Reactant] is the concentration of the reactant
- [\text{Photosensitensitator}] is the concentration of the photosensitizer
- Intensity is the intensity of the incident light
- Duration is the duration of exposure to the light

The effect of concentration and duration of exposure of a photosensitizer on the rate of photochemical reactions can be analyzed as follows:

1. Concentration of Photosensitizer:
