- k is the rate constant
- [Reactant] is the concentration of the reactant
- [\text{Photosensitensitator}] is the concentration of the photosensitizer
- Intensity is the intensity of the incident light
- Duration is the duration of exposure to the light

The effect of concentration and duration of exposure of a photosensitizer on the rate of photochemical reactions can be analyzed as follows:

1. Concentration of Photosensitizer:
The rate of a photochemical reaction is proportional to the concentration of the photosensitizer. This is because the photosensitizer absorbs the light and then transfers the energy to the reactants. A higher concentration of photosensitizer results in more absorption of light and more energy transfer to the reactants, leading to a faster reaction rate.

2. Duration of Exposure:
The rate of a photochemical reaction is proportional to the duration of exposure to the light. This is because the reaction proceeds over time, and a longer exposure period allows for more energy transfer from the photosensitizer to the reactants, leading to a faster reaction rate.

