- The reduction potential of the metal oxide determines whether it can be reduced by carbon.  
- The reduction potential is the potential of the reaction:  
  \[ \text{Ox} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{M} \]  
- For reduction by carbon, the reduction potential must be negative than the reduction potential of carbon.  
- The reduction potentials of the given metal oxides are:  
  - Al₂O₃:  \[ \text{AlO}_3 + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al} + \text{O}_2 \] (Reduction potential is negative, so Al₂O₃ can be reduced)  
  - PbO:  \( \text{PbO} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb} + \text{O}_2 \) (Reduction potential is negative, so PbO can be reduced)  
  - ZnO:  \( \text{ZnO} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn} + \text{O}_2 \) (Reduction potential is positive, so ZnO cannot be reduced)  
  - Fe₂O₃:  \( \text{FeO}_3 + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe} + \text{O}_2 \) (Reduction potential is negative, so Fe₂O₃ can be reduced)  

