","To find the probability that we get heads in at least 9 of the 12 flips, we can use the binomial probability formula. The probability of getting exactly \( k \) heads in \( n \) flips is given by:
\[
P(k) = \binom{n}{k} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^k \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{n-k} = \binom{n}{k} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^n
\]
Here, \( n = 12 \) and the probability of heads is \( p = 0.5 \). We want to find the probability of getting at least 9 heads, which is the sum of the probabilities of getting 9, 10, 11, or 12 heads:
\[
P(k \geq 9) = P(9) + P(10) + P(11) + P(12) = \binom{12}{9} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12} + \binom{12}{10} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12} + \binom{12}{11} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12} + \binom{12}{12} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{12}
\]
Calculating the binomial coefficients:
\[
\binom{12}{9} = \frac{12!}{9!3!} = \frac{12 \times 11 \times 10}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = 12 \times 5 \times frac{1}{2} = 60
\]
\[
\binom{12}{10} = \frac{12!}{10!2!} = \frac{12 \times 11}{2 \times 1} = 66
\]
\[
\binom{12}{11} = \frac{12!}{11!1!} = 12
\]
\[
\binom{12}{12} = 1
\]
Now, summing the probabilities:
\[
P(k \geq 9) = \frac{60 + 66 + 12 + 1}{2^{12}} = \frac{149}{4096} \approx 0.0364
\]
Thus, the probability is:
\[
\boxed{\dfrac{149}{4096}}
\]",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
