First, let's find the time experienced by the clock in the cockpit relative to Earth. Since the clock in the cockpit is in the same frame of reference as the spaceship, its time experienced by Earth is given by the time dilation formula:

t' = t / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where t' is the time measured by the observer on Earth, t is the time measured by the clock on the spaceship, and v is the speed of the spaceship relative to Earth.

We are given that the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth. This is the time t measured by the clock on the spaceship. We are also given that the speed of the spaceship is 0.6c. Plugging these values into the time dilation formula:

