This is derived from the Clausius-Clouville relation for irreversible processes:
\[
\frac{dS}{dT} = \frac{1}{T} \left( \frac{\partial S}{\partial V} \frac{dV}{dT} + \frac{\partial S}{\partial p} \frac{dp}{dT} \right)
\]
For a system with entropy \( S \), pressure \( p \), and volume \( V \), the entropy production rate is:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{1}{T} \left( \frac{\partial S}{\partial V} \frac{dV}{dT} + \frac{\partial S}{\partial p} \frac{dp}{dT} \right)
\]
For irreversible processes, entropy increases, and \( \Pi_S > 0 \).

In the case of friction, the work done by the friction force \( F_{\text{fr}} \) is dissipated as heat, leading to entropy production. The entropy production rate is:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}
\]
Here, \( F_{\text{fr}} \) is the friction force, \( v \) is the mean relative velocity of the two hands, and \( T \) is the room temperature. The work done by the friction force is \( F_{\text{fr}} v \), and this work is converted into heat, which increases the entropy of the surroundings. 

