A water pump designed to deliver 10 liters of water per second is experiencing failure due to cavitation. Determine the minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation from occurring inside the pump. Assume the temperature of the water to be 20°C and that the inlet pipe of the pump is located at a depth of 5 meters below the water surface.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To determine the minimum inlet pressure required to prevent cavitation, we need to compare the static pressure at the inlet to the vapor pressure of water at the given temperature. Cavitation occurs when the pressure drops below the vapor pressure, causing water to change from liquid to vapor.

1. **Calculate the static pressure at the inlet**:
   The static pressure at the inlet is the sum of the atmospheric pressure and the pressure due to the depth of the pipe.
   \[
   P_{\text{static}} = P_{\text{atm}} + \rho g h
   \]
   - \( P_{\text{atm}} = 101325 \, \text{Pa} \) (standard atmospheric pressure)
   - \( \rho = 998 \, \text{kg/m}^3 \) (density of water at 20°C)
   - \( g = 9.81 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity)
   - \( h = 5 \, \text{m} \) (depth of the pipe)

   Substituting the values:
   \[
   P_{\text{static}} = 101325 + (998 \times 9.81 \times 5) = 101325 + 49440 = 150765 \, \text{Pa}
   \]

