","To solve this problem, we need to consider the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a high speed relative to an observer. The time experienced by the moving observer is shorter than the time experienced by the stationary observer.

First, let's find the time experienced by the clock in the cockpit relative to Earth. Since the clock in the cockpit is in the same frame of reference as the spaceship, its time experienced by Earth is given by the time dilation formula:

t' = t / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where t' is the time measured by the observer on Earth, t is the time measured by the clock on the spaceship, and v is the speed of the spaceship relative to Earth.

We are given that the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth. This is the time t measured by the clock on the spaceship. We are also given that the speed of the spaceship is 0.6c. Plugging these values into the time dilation formula:

t' = 3 / sqrt(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
t' = 3 / sqrt(1 - 0.36)
t' = 3 / sqrt(0.64)
t' = 3 / 0.8
t' = 3.75 years

So, the clock on Earth shows a time of 3.75 years when the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years.

Now, we need to find the time shown by the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth. Since the clock in the cargo box is in the same frame of reference as the clock in the cockpit, it will experience the same time dilation effect. Therefore, the time measured by the clock in the cargo bay relative to Earth will be the same as the time measured by the clock in the cockpit relative to Earth.

