\[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} \]  
First, we need to find the height of the triangle. Since the triangle is isosceles, the altitude from the right angle to the base divides the base into two equal parts. Thus, the base is divided into two segments of length \( \frac{20}{2} = 10 \).  
Now, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the height \( h \):  
\[ h^2 + 10^2 = 15^2 \]  
\[ h^2 + 100 = 225 \]  
\[ h^2 = 225 - 100 \]  
\[ h^2 = 125 \]  
